{"kind":"request","text":"Separate what an act describes from what it was reasoned from, so world-staleness stops dragging healthy current work.","body":{"conditions":"Discovered while implementing the ratified convention, and recorded in the assert this rests on: rests_on carries two relations that staleness cannot tell apart. One is what an act describes, where a death downstream means go and re-read. The other is what an act was reasoned from, where a death means only that the reasoning happened at an earlier state. Superseding a predecessor artifact, which rule two requires, therefore flared the artifact for the current head, its readiness report, and the open review request, because each rested on evidence that cited the retired artifact.\n\nDecide before implementing, and decide by proposal and ratification rather than in passing, because this changes a convention that is already ratified and normative for every actor. The assert names four candidates: distinguish the edge so that a describes relation is separate from a general rests_on and only the first propagates world-staleness; stop world-staleness propagating through statements that are not artifacts, keeping the direct case and losing multi-hop reach; make it a citation practice rather than a mechanism; or accept the drag on the ground that a flare means re-check rather than wrong. My own view, which is not a decision, is that distinguishing the edge is the honest fix and the largest, and that the multi-hop case is worth keeping because it catches real drift.\n\nWhatever lands must keep the property the convention was ratified for: a document describing behaviour still flares when that behaviour changes. Demonstrate both directions on the real log rather than only on fixtures — a case that must flare and a case that must not — since the fixtures are what let the original gap survive. Update SKILL.md discipline 8 and the AGENTS.md loop to match. If the answer touches the schema, say plainly what existing history means under the new reading, because every act already written used the single edge.\n\nSequenced before the documentation set request, for the same reason the convention itself was: pages will cite both the artifacts for the behaviour they document and the decisions they were written from, so anchoring prose before this is settled would have to be redone. The flare test named in that request would otherwise pass for the wrong reason.\n\nObtain independent exact-head review before merge, from an actor whose fingerprint differs from the implementer.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}